ID: B2-Ch10a-Intro
TEXT:

 

"Hey, Kate!" Hadrik calls out. "Over here for a second! We've got someone you should meet!"

Kate steps out of her tent, blinking against the dawn light. It has been nearly an hour since Daren's old teacher walked into our camp. Once we sorted out that he had not come to slit throats, we told him Daren would be awake before long and dealt him into a round of Met-Zek.

We spent the hour trading cards while Nolderan ran through stories from the years he spent drilling Daren in swordplay and enchanting. The ex-sage agreed to the traditional rules without blinking, putting away a full bottle of dwarven ale in the time it took to play three hands. He carries it with the same stubborn constitution as Hadrik, showing no sign that the liquor has reached his head.

"Who is this?" Kate asks, slowing her pace as she nears the fire pit.

"Remember Daren talking about the sparring match that gave him the scar on his forehead?" Hadrik says. "This is the master he meant. Nolderan. He stopped by to see Daren after ten years apart."

"A pleasure, my lady," Nolderan says, offering a formal bow.

"Save the pleasantries," Kate says, her voice cold. "Say what you mean. I'm not bothering to suppress my aura right now. If you taught Daren, you're the one who taught him how to hunt banshees."

"You're thinking of his other master," Nolderan says with an easy wave of his hand. "The white mage. We both held sage titles once, so the mix-up is natural enough. I assure you, I have no quarrel with banshees. If these people trust you, I have no reason to treat you as an enemy."

"She isn't a banshee," Hadrik cuts in. "She's a female mage."

Nolderan's eyebrows lift. "Now that is something you don't run across every day."

"How did you know where to find Daren?" Kate asks.

"I didn't know he was in this particular stretch of woods," Nolderan says. "I was close enough to catch his aura and decided to drop in."

"You just happened to be close enough?" Kate's eyes narrow. "Wandering alone through the forest in the dead of night?"

"My camp isn't far," Nolderan says. "I took a scouting rotation on the night watch and swept the perimeter. I see how that looks from where you're standing, though."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Intro2
TEXT:

"Of course I'm suspicious," Kate says. "We have no proof you even know Daren. For all we know, someone hired you to put a blade in his back."

"If it's proof you want, ask the man himself," Nolderan says, nodding past her shoulder. "He's on his way over right now."

"Master Nolderan?" Daren stops dead a few paces away, staring. "Is that really you? You've gone gray!"

"Ten years since we spoke, and that's the greeting I get?" Nolderan sets his cards down on the log, grinning as he rises to his feet. "If you made time to visit your old instructor now and then, a head of gray hair wouldn't come as such a shock."

"I'm sorry," Daren says, lowering his head. "I've been occupied with--"

"With saving the world, yes, yes. I know." Nolderan rolls his eyes. "You never could take a joke, boy. I hear plenty about your exploits. Stop looking like you're attending a funeral and give your teacher a hug."

He pulls Daren into a rough embrace, hammering three heavy thuds against Daren's shoulder blades.

"Still clanking around in full plate, I see," Nolderan says, stepping back to rap his knuckles against Daren's breastplate. "Don't you ever tire of hauling that metal heap through the dirt? What good are all the footwork drills I gave you if the weight cuts your speed in half?"

"We've had this argument," Daren says. "The plate is the only reliable defense I have against physical blows. I'm keeping it on."

"Why bother absorbing a strike when you can step around it?" Nolderan taps his own forehead. "Do I need to carve a third line between your eyes before the lesson sticks?"

"Let's not do this," Daren groans. "We haven't seen each other in over a decade. Can we celebrate the reunion instead of picking at old fights?"

"Fair enough!" Nolderan unhooks a fresh bottle of dwarven ale from his belt. "Let's drink to it, then."

"Dwarven ale?" Daren's brow furrows. "At dawn? We could be ambushed at any moment."

"Predictable as always," Nolderan sighs. He brings two bottles together with a sharp glass ring. "I'll clink them for you, then, and pretend you have some spirit in you. To your health, Daren."

He pulls the cork with his teeth and drinks deep.

"The rest of you are welcome to join," Nolderan says, glancing toward Hadrik, Kate, and me. "No point letting good ale go to waste."

choice("Agree to drink with Nolderan", B2-Ch10a-Charmed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Charmed, v_b2_ch10_toast = 1)
choice("Refuse Nolderan's offer", B2-Ch10a-Charmed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Charmed, v_b2_ch10_toast = 0)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Charmed
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 1 && v_b2_ch9_drink == 1) {
"Sure, I'll join," I say. "Pass the bottle."

"Good man," Nolderan says, handing me the unopened one. "Anyone else?"

"I'm already holding a bottle, so count me in," Hadrik says, stepping up to the circle.

"I'll pass," Kate says flatly.

"A toast, then!" Nolderan lifts his bottle high, and Hadrik and I raise ours to meet it. "To survival and good fortune, friends. My thanks to all of you for keeping this stubborn pupil of mine in one piece. He's hard-headed, but his heart is in the right place."

"Cheers," Hadrik and I say together, knocking our bottles together before taking a long pull.

The heavy, smoky brew hits the back of my throat. As I lower the bottle, the room tilts slightly under my feet. Between the card-game drinking during my shift and this fresh mouthful, the ale is catching up with me fast. A dull warmth hums behind my temples. I just need to sweat it out before anything tries to kill us.

"What's all the shouting?" Flower asks, rubbing her eyes as she and Leila wander out from their tents. "Did we get a visitor?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 1 && v_b2_ch9_drink == 0) {
"Sure, I'll join," I say. "Pass the bottle."

"Good man," Nolderan says, handing me the unopened one. "Anyone else?"

"I'm already holding a bottle, so count me in," Hadrik says, stepping up to the circle.

"I'll pass," Kate says flatly.

"A toast, then!" Nolderan lifts his bottle high, and Hadrik and I raise ours to meet it. "To survival and good fortune, friends. My thanks to all of you for keeping this stubborn pupil of mine in one piece. He's hard-headed, but his heart is in the right place."

"Cheers," Hadrik and I say together, knocking our bottles together before taking a long pull.

"What's all the shouting?" Flower asks, rubbing her eyes as she and Leila wander out from their tents. "Did we get a visitor?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 0) {
"I'll pass, but thanks for the offer," I say.

"I'll pass as well," Kate says.

"How about you, Hadrik?" Nolderan asks. "You're not going to leave an old warrior drinking alone, are you?"

"I've still got half a bottle in my fist," Hadrik grins, stepping forward. "Count me in."

"Good!" Nolderan sets the spare bottle on a stump and raises his own. "A toast, then! To survival and good fortune, friends. My thanks to all of you for keeping this stubborn pupil of mine in one piece. He's hard-headed, but his heart is in the right place."

"Cheers!" Hadrik says, clinking glass against glass before drinking.

"What's all the shouting?" Flower asks, rubbing her eyes as she and Leila wander out from their tents. "Did we get a visitor?"

}
"This is Daren's old enchanting master," Hadrik explains, gesturing with his bottle. "First time they've crossed paths in ten years, so we're marking the occasion."

"Another female mage?" Nolderan asks, his gaze locking onto Flower as he reads her aura.

"Sort of," Hadrik says. "Flower is the mage, but there's a banshee sharing the body with her named Illuna. When the banshee takes over, the girl's eyes go bright blue. Takes a bit to get used to, but you figure it out."

"I see," Nolderan murmurs, studying her.

"Nice to meet you!" Flower says, beaming.

"And over here is Leila," Hadrik adds. "She's a lessathi."

"A what?" Nolderan tilts his head.

"The locals call them the ancients," Hadrik says. "Does that ring a bell?"

"Ah, yes," Nolderan nods slowly. "An old bloodline known for crafting complex magical apparatus."

"That's the one," Hadrik says. "Leila is one of their descendants."

Glowing blue letters materialize in the air before us: Charmed.

Nolderan blinks at the shimmering script. "Well now..."

"Forgot to mention," Hadrik says. "Leila doesn't speak. She writes everything out in midair."

"A handy trick," Nolderan says, turning toward her with a respectful nod. "Pleasure to meet you as well, young lady."

"Master," Daren says, stepping between them. "I'm glad to see you well, but what brings you to Varathia? I thought you were done with mage tournaments after... after what happened."

"After I lost the sage title, you mean?" Nolderan chuckles, shaking his head. "No need to tiptoe around it, boy. It was fourteen years ago. I've long made my peace with it. I haven't entered many rings since, but this tournament is different. The Magium offers something no other competition can match. I couldn't pass it by."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Undefeated, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Undefeated)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Undefeated
TEXT:

"Sorry for interrupting," Flower says, "but why didn't you try to win your title back if it meant so much to you? Don't each of the continents hold a tournament every year?"

"Are you from Varathia by any chance, little girl?" Nolderan asks.

"Yes, I was born here," Flower says. "Why do you ask?"

"That explains the question," Nolderan says. "Word from the outside world doesn't make its way onto this island very often, does it?"

"We get occasional travelers, but not many," Flower says. "Did they cancel the yearly competitions? Is that why you stopped entering?"

"No, the tournaments go on as scheduled," Nolderan says. "It's the man who took the title from me. After fighting him, I knew I would never hold it again."

"What do you mean?" Flower asks.

"The four current sages are nothing like the ones who held the title in past generations," Daren says. "They belong in a category of their own. In the past, a sage rarely held a title for more than two or three years before another master unseated them. Three of the current four have stood undefeated for over a decade. Azarius, the Sage of the West, has held his seat for more than thirty years. His likeness is printed across Western broadsheets every week. A man his age should be slowing down, but every year he enters the ring stronger than the last. The matches are treated as mere ceremonies now. Most people assume Azarius will hold the seat until the day he dies."

"Daren's always had an obsession with Azarius, but the other three are just as terrifying," Hadrik says. "Talmak, the Sage of the North, is the most destructive earth elementalist alive. In twelve years, no challenger has pushed him hard enough to make him sweat. The only man who gave him a real contest was the previous sage, and he's buried in the dirt along with every other fool who stepped into the arena against him. Then you have Selgurd. The elf. Don't get me started on the elf..."

"Has he held the title for a long time as well?" Flower asks.

"No, but the sage he killed did," Hadrik says. "The previous Sage of the East was considered equal to the long-standing three, untouchable for years. Then this unknown elf walks out of the crowd and ends him in under sixty seconds. Nobody even saw the strike. Selgurd cast a heavy illusion over the arena, blocking out sight and sound. When the mist cleared, the old sage lay dead on the stone, and the entire stadium went dead quiet. That was only last year. The judges launched an inquiry to figure out how he died, but they turned up nothing. The elf refused to name the spell, but the working theory is that he shredded the old man's mind from the inside."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Drakesbane, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Drakesbane, v_available_points = +3, v_available_points_aux = +3)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Drakesbane
TEXT:

"There were no marks on the body," Daren says. "If it was mental manipulation, it must have been something like the nightmare ritual the ogre shaman trapped us in back at the fort, where dying in the illusion stops your heart. The researchers who combed the arena claimed the residual aura matched no known spell on record."

"Whether the tournament organizers covered up the details or not," Hadrik says, "the elf took the seat of the Sage of the East, and plenty of people are furious about it. Whatever trick he used, Selgurd executed one of the greatest mages of our time without breaking a sweat. He's a monster."

"What about the Sage of the South?" Flower asks.

"The Sage of the South is the one who took my title fourteen years ago," Nolderan says. "I was outmatched from the opening bell. He remains undefeated to this day, and no challenger has ever brought him close to defeat. He goes by Drakesbane, a legendary dragon hunter. Nobody knows his true name, but the moniker stuck thanks to the massive hall of dragon skulls he keeps in his estate. True dragons rarely leave their mountain nests anymore, but Drakesbane hunts them in their own lairs for sport. Skeptics claim the heads belong to overgrown wyverns to keep from admitting dragons still roam the wild, but anyone who has stood within five paces of a dragon's skull knows the difference immediately."

A sharp chime rings out from Leila's pocket, followed by an identical tone from mine. We pull our stat devices out together. The glowing display shows three unassigned points ready to allocate.

choice("Invest points now", B2-Ch10a-Stats-spent, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Stats-spent, special:stats)
choice("Invest points later(by pressing the stats button on the top of the screen)", B2-Ch10a-Fortune, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Fortune)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Stats-spent
TEXT:

"There were no marks on the body," Daren says. "If it was mental manipulation, it must have been something like the nightmare ritual the ogre shaman trapped us in back at the fort, where dying in the illusion stops your heart. The researchers who combed the arena claimed the residual aura matched no known spell on record."

"Whether the tournament organizers covered up the details or not," Hadrik says, "the elf took the seat of the Sage of the East, and plenty of people are furious about it. Whatever trick he used, Selgurd executed one of the greatest mages of our time without breaking a sweat. He's a monster."

"What about the Sage of the South?" Flower asks.

"The Sage of the South is the one who took my title fourteen years ago," Nolderan says. "I was outmatched from the opening bell. He remains undefeated to this day, and no challenger has ever brought him close to defeat. He goes by Drakesbane, a legendary dragon hunter. Nobody knows his true name, but the moniker stuck thanks to the massive hall of dragon skulls he keeps in his estate. True dragons rarely leave their mountain nests anymore, but Drakesbane hunts them in their own lairs for sport. Skeptics claim the heads belong to overgrown wyverns to keep from admitting dragons still roam the wild, but anyone who has stood within five paces of a dragon's skull knows the difference immediately."

A sharp chime rings out from Leila's pocket, followed by an identical tone from mine. We pull our stat devices out together. The glowing display shows three unassigned points ready to allocate.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Fortune, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Fortune)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Fortune
TEXT:

"Did you get three as well?" I ask Leila.

"Yes, I put them all into reflexes," Leila writes in floating blue script as we slide the devices back into our pockets.

"Were those... fortune-teller charms?" Nolderan asks, staring at our hands.

"They look like it," I say.

"They don't tell fortunes," Daren explains. "They channel magic to amplify the bearer's physical attributes. It sounds impossible, but I've watched them work firsthand."

"Remarkable," Nolderan says, rubbing his chin. "I took them for cheap trinkets sold in market stalls. How much power do they provide?"

"It scales with the bearer's baseline physical condition," Leila writes. "A weaker body draws less enhancement from the device."

"Understood," Nolderan says.

"What about you, Master?" Daren asks. "What have you been doing these past few years? I heard talk that you took up mercenary contracts, but I didn't want to believe it."

"The rumors are true," Nolderan says with a dry chuckle. "Losing a sage title so quickly, and so brutally, ruined my standing as an instructor. Potential students assumed I was washed up and took their coin to other enchanting and martial masters. The Southern Continent has no shortage of academies. When the commissions dried up, I took my swords where people were willing to pay. Mercenary clients don't care about your reputation in high society. They only care whether you finish the job. It isn't glorious work, but it keeps bread on the table."

"I'm sorry," Daren says, looking down. "I had no idea things were so difficult. I should have stayed in contact."

"Save your guilt," Nolderan says. "A warrior's life has its peaks and valleys. This tournament offers a path back to where I was. That's why we're all here, isn't it? To take hold of our own fate?"

"Actually," Daren says, "my reason for entering is--"

"Pardon the intrusion," Arraka's voice cuts in from the golden amulet around Flower's neck, dripping with sarcasm. "Illuna gave me strict orders to announce when hostiles attempt to encircle the camp, and as it happens, that is precisely what is unfolding around us right this second."

"Monsters again?" Daren grips the hilt of his sword.

"Monsters," Arraka confirms. "A larger pack this time. They're trying to suppress their auras, but I recognize the orc and the two ogres who fled two days ago. They went and rounded up friends. No sign of the two goblins who rescued them last time, so they must think they have enough muscle to crush you without help. Seventeen warriors and five mages. Five ogres in total, twelve orcs including the captain with the flaming axes. The mages are a mix of orcs and ogres."

"Master," Daren turns to Nolderan. "You need to leave. These beasts came to wipe out our group. If you slip through the trees now, they will ignore you and concentrate on us. I regret cutting our reunion short, but if we both survive this tournament, our paths will cross again."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Flank, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Flank)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Flank
TEXT:

"What are you talking about, boy?" Nolderan snorts. "You think I'm going to run off and leave my best student to get butchered minutes after we found each other?"

"These aren't wild beasts," Daren says. "They're Tyrath's vanguard. This won't be a simple skirmish."

"Bah!" Nolderan waves a dismissive hand. "You gave me the same speech before we cleared that nest of trolls and ogres in the southern crags. You complained the whole climb."

"I was nineteen!" Daren says, exasperated. "Three months into training, and you dragged me into a cavern hunt. I still don't know how you talked me into staying."

"The training worked, didn't it?" Nolderan draws two slender steel blades from his hips, spinning them with practiced ease. "Sparring in a yard doesn't make a warrior. Blood and iron do."

"How close are they?" Hadrik asks, narrowing his eyes at the tree line.

"Closing fast," Kate says. "They gave up trying to cross the open clearing ahead. No cover. They're circling through the timber to hit our flanks and rear."

"Good," Hadrik grunts. "Keeps the fight away from the tents. We're days from the nearest market if our canvas gets shredded."

"Remember the formation from two days ago," Daren commands, his voice dropping into battlefield rhythm. "Leila, hold your position on the line. Don't dive in alone. Kate, stick to your targets."

"Understood," Kate says.

She raises both hands. Four jagged icicles tear through the air, punching into the thick canopy on our left. Two ogres collapse into the brush with heavy thuds. The three surviving brutes roar, crashing through the saplings in a full charge.

With their flank exposed, the orcs on our right step out from behind the trunks. They lock their heavy iron shields edge-to-edge, maces gripped tight, advancing in a steady wall.

Kate launches a second spray of ice at the charging ogres, but the monsters swing massive spiked clubs to shatter the shards mid-flight.

"Flower!" Daren calls out. "Take the sky. Drop fire on the ogres before they reach our line."

"On it!" Flower launches herself upward, twin jets of bright orange flame blasting from the soles of her feet.

"Leave the orcs to us," Daren adds. "Their armor is lined against elemental magic. Weapons only."

Above the clearing, Flower rains fireballs into the charging ogres. The explosions tear up the turf, forcing the beasts to stumble and cover their faces. The opening is all Kate needs. A pair of razor-sharp ice spears drives through their skulls, dropping all three into the mud.

"Ogres are down," Daren shouts. "Break the shield wall, then we sweep the woods for the casters."

choice("Use the crossbow to fight the orcs", B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_b2_ch10_crossbow = 1)
choice("Use the scimitar to fight the orcs", B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_b2_ch10_crossbow = 0) if (v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0)
choice("Use the dagger to fight the orcs", B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Splitting, v_b2_ch10_crossbow = 0) if (v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Splitting
TEXT:

The orcs split into two files, shields locked tight, marching in from both sides of the clearing. The captain with the flaming axes is nowhere in sight.

"I'll take the left flank," Nolderan says, already gliding forward over the grass.

"Five warriors on that side," Daren says, raising his shield and falling in behind his teacher. "I'm with you. Hadrik can break the right."

He glances back at Leila, who stands with her daggers drawn, waiting for an order.

"Leila, back up Hadrik," Daren says. "Master Nolderan and I have this side covered."

Leila nods once, breaking into a sprint beside Hadrik toward the right line.

"I'll hunt down the mages," Kate says, stepping toward the treeline.

"Hold!" Daren calls out over his shoulder. "We don't split up in the woods. They could have ward-traps waiting. We clear the field together first."

Kate lowers her hand. "Understood."

Hadrik reaches the right shield wall first. He plants his boots and drives a straight punch into the center shield. The heavy metal buckles inward with a deafening crack, throwing the orc bearer backward into the dirt. Hadrik leaps immediately, crashing down with a hammer fist onto the next shield in line.

The impact rings through the trees. Bone snaps inside the warrior's forearm under the sheer pressure. Before the injured orc can roll clear, Hadrik grabs him by both ankles and swings him like a club into two adjacent warriors, sending all three sprawling across the grass.

On the left, Daren and Nolderan split to flank their five targets, forcing the orcs to break formation and turn outward. Nolderan moves like a shadow, weaving inside the swing of a heavy mace without letting steel touch his clothes. Daren anchors the opposite side, taking crushing blows on his enchanted kite shield and stepping forward to drive his broadsword into exposed ribs.

Even after a decade apart, their coordination is seamless. They trade targets without a word, covering blind spots and shifting angles as if running a practiced drill. When Nolderan slips behind an enemy, Daren already has the counter-swing baited and parried.

"Just like old times, Daren!" Nolderan yells, his twin blades carving clean through an orc's neck.

"You're reckless as ever!" Daren grunts, catching a spiked mace on the rim of his shield and shoving the warrior back. "I don't know how you survived this long!"

"Admit it, you missed this!" Nolderan laughs, dancing around a clumsy thrust. "Watch this one, boy!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Crossbow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Crossbow) if (v_b2_ch10_crossbow == 1)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Dagger, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Dagger) if (v_b2_ch10_crossbow == 0)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Crossbow
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 1 && v_b2_ch9_drink == 1) {
Nolderan sprints across the turf toward an orc locked in combat with Daren. In full stride, the ex-sage launches into a horizontal leap, twisting mid-air to slam both boots square into the monster's spine. The orc stumbles forward off-balance as Nolderan hits the grass on his shoulder. Daren drives his broadsword down through the warrior's collar before the brute can turn. Nolderan rolls clear of a counter-swing from a second orc, springs to his feet, and fakes a wide overhead slash with his left blade. As the orc jerks his shield up to intercept, Nolderan's right sword whips around in a low arc, shearing clean through the warrior's neck.

The haze of dwarven ale is still buzzing in the back of my skull, but standing idle isn't an option.

I bring my crossbow up, centering the sights on one of the warriors pressing Leila on the right flank.

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 0 || v_b2_ch9_drink == 0) {
Nolderan sprints across the turf toward an orc locked in combat with Daren. In full stride, the ex-sage launches into a horizontal leap, twisting mid-air to slam both boots square into the monster's spine. The orc stumbles forward off-balance as Nolderan hits the grass on his shoulder. Daren drives his broadsword down through the warrior's collar before the brute can turn. Nolderan rolls clear of a counter-swing from a second orc, springs to his feet, and fakes a wide overhead slash with his left blade. As the orc jerks his shield up to intercept, Nolderan's right sword whips around in a low arc, shearing clean through the warrior's neck.

I bring my crossbow up, centering the sights on one of the warriors pressing Leila on the right flank.

}
#if(v_combat_technique >= 2) {
Tracking a moving target over this distance takes precision, but my combat training lets me read the cadence of the orc's footwork. As he shifts his weight to raise his mace, I squeeze the trigger. The bolt streaks across the clearing and punches straight through the narrow eye-slit of his helmet. The warrior drops like a stone.

Across the field, Hadrik crushes the final standing orc against the dirt. The team forms up near the treeline to push into the woods after the mages and their captain.

"Short work of the dragon's finest," Nolderan laughs, flicking dark blood from his blades.

"We aren't done," Daren warns. "Five casters wait in the timber, and their captain is far deadlier than these foot soldiers. Move in before they--"

A deep, deafening horn blast shatters the air from deep within the woods. The sound reverberates in our chests, forcing us to clap our hands over our ears.

"Up!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "I missed the sky! Wyvern riders diving from the clouds!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_crossbow == 1 && v_combat_technique == 1) {
Tracking a moving target over this distance is tricky, but my combat training helps me anticipate his next step. The bolt flies true. It catches the rim of the visor rather than the eye-slit, but the reinforced limbs of my upgraded crossbow pack enough punch to drive the steel point straight through the plate. The orc collapses instantly.

Across the field, Hadrik crushes the final standing orc against the dirt. The team forms up near the treeline to push into the woods after the mages and their captain.

"Short work of the dragon's finest," Nolderan laughs, flicking dark blood from his blades.

"We aren't done," Daren warns. "Five casters wait in the timber, and their captain is far deadlier than these foot soldiers. Move in before they--"

A deep, deafening horn blast shatters the air from deep within the woods. The sound reverberates in our chests, forcing us to clap our hands over our ears.

"Up!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "I missed the sky! Wyvern riders diving from the clouds!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_crossbow == 0 && v_combat_technique == 1) {
Tracking a moving target over this distance is tricky, but my combat training helps me time the shot. The bolt strikes the thick curve of the orc's helmet with a loud metallic ring, glancing off without piercing the steel. The impact jerks the warrior's head back. That split-second stagger is all Leila needs. She darts under his raised guard and draws her enchanted dagger across his throat.

Across the field, Hadrik crushes the final standing orc against the dirt. The team forms up near the treeline to push into the woods after the mages and their captain.

"Short work of the dragon's finest," Nolderan laughs, flicking dark blood from his blades.

"We aren't done," Daren warns. "Five casters wait in the timber, and their captain is far deadlier than these foot soldiers. Move in before they--"

A deep, deafening horn blast shatters the air from deep within the woods. The sound reverberates in our chests, forcing us to clap our hands over our ears.

"Up!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "I missed the sky! Wyvern riders diving from the clouds!"

}
#if(v_combat_technique == 0) {
I try to lead the target, but the bolt flies wide, thudding harmlessly into an oak trunk yards away. Firing into close-quarters melee without better aim risks hitting Leila. I lower the crossbow just as Leila slips behind the warrior's guard, severing his jugular with a swift upward slice of her dagger.

Across the field, Hadrik crushes the final standing orc against the dirt. The team forms up near the treeline to push into the woods after the mages and their captain.

"Short work of the dragon's finest," Nolderan laughs, flicking dark blood from his blades.

"We aren't done," Daren warns. "Five casters wait in the timber, and their captain is far deadlier than these foot soldiers. Move in before they--"

A deep, deafening horn blast shatters the air from deep within the woods. The sound reverberates in our chests, forcing us to clap our hands over our ears.

"Up!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "I missed the sky! Wyvern riders diving from the clouds!"

}
Ten black wyverns plunge from the low cloud layer, black leathery wings tucked tight. Each beast carries an orc in boiled leather armed with heavy lances or repeating crossbows. Wyverns are leaner cousins to true dragons, fifteen feet snout to tail with twenty-foot wingspans, lacking fire breath and higher intelligence, but fast and lethal in a dive. Seeing that they've lost the element of surprise, the riders flare their wings and begin circling overhead.

"Flower, Illuna, and I will handle the riders," Kate says, stepping forward. "None of you can reach them in the air. Push into the woods and cut down the summoners before they call in more troops."

"Agreed," Daren says, raising his blade. "At least we forced out their reserve. They won't catch us off-guard again--"

A harsh blue light erupts beneath our boots. An intricate runic circle twenty paces wide burns into the grass, glowing brighter with every pulse.

"Out of the circle!" Daren roars, shoving backward. "Move! Now!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Mammoth, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Mammoth)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Dagger
TEXT:

