choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_b2_ch10_weakpoint == 0 && v_strength < 4 || v_b2_ch10_weakpoint == 1 && v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0 && v_reflexes < 2)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Arena, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Arena) if (v_b2_ch10_weakpoint == 0 && v_strength == 4 || v_b2_ch10_weakpoint == 1 && v_reflexes >= 2 || v_b2_ch10_weakpoint == 1 && v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Arena
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch10_mammoth,1)
achievement("Mammoth slayer",v_ac_b2_ch10_mammoth)
I touch down on the trampled grass beside the fallen beast. Hadrik lets out a booming laugh, wiping a sleeve across a swelling black eye.

"Now that's a proper scrap!" Hadrik shouts, slamming a heavy fist into his palm. "Moments like that are why I entered this tournament! Never a dull day traveling with this pack."

"A clean combination from the two of you," Nolderan says, sheathing his swords. "I had no idea either of you could handle aerial combat."

"Resourcefulness is Barry's specialty," Hadrik says. "He proved that back in Thilias when he dragged himself out of the arena alive against impossible odds."

"An arena?" Nolderan's eyebrows tick up.

"Long story," Hadrik says. "The arena master stacked the matches against Barry and Leila, giving them dead weight for teammates, but they still came out on top. We'll give you the full tale over a drink later."

"I only hope the captain isn't gathering another strike force," Daren says, scanning the treeline. "If they keep teleporting out when the fight turns against them, they'll wear us down through attrition."

"Then do a better job concealing your trail," Nolderan says. "If they tracked you twice in three days, you're leaving a path a blind goblin could follow."

"You're right," Daren sighs. "They must have teleported to our previous camp and tracked our bootprints through the pine needle beds."

choice(""What if they have a tracking spell cast on us?"", B2-Ch10a-Checked, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Checked, v_b2_ch10_scouts = 0)
choice(""They could also have scouts, scattered all around the continent."", B2-Ch10a-Checked, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Checked, v_b2_ch10_scouts = 1)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Checked
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_bribe == 2 && v_b2_ch10_scouts == 0 && v_ch10_gems == 1) {
"What if they have a tracking spell cast on us?"

"Like the ward the golden fox placed on us outside Thilias?" Daren shakes his head. "Unlikely. As a white mage, lingering enchantments stand out to me immediately. The fox is an ancient spirit whose weaves are nearly invisible, but orc shamans don't have that subtlety. I would catch their tracking runes within seconds."

"Look above," Nolderan says, pointing toward the canopy. "Your elementalists are descending. Wait, one wyvern is still in the air. Coming straight down. Draw steel!"

"Hold!" I yell, stepping in front of him. "He's with us!"

"He is?" Daren lowers his broadsword, confused.

"I bought his contract mid-flight with a ruby," I say.

"Ha!" Hadrik bellows, clapping me on the shoulder. "What did I say? The man has a bottomless bag of insane solutions. Who else tries to bribe an enemy rider while falling through the sky?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_bribe == 2 && v_b2_ch10_scouts == 1 && v_ch10_gems == 1) {
"They could also have scouts, scattered all around the continent."

"That would explain the timing," Daren says. "Tyrath has thousands of orcs in his ranks. Scouts with transceivers could report our coordinates before we ever spot them. We have no choice but to break their vanguards until they realize the cost is too high."

"Look above," Nolderan says, pointing toward the canopy. "Your elementalists are descending. Wait, one wyvern is still in the air. Coming straight down. Draw steel!"

"Hold!" I yell, stepping in front of him. "He's with us!"

"He is?" Daren lowers his broadsword, confused.

"I bought his contract mid-flight with a ruby," I say.

"Ha!" Hadrik bellows, clapping me on the shoulder. "What did I say? The man has a bottomless bag of insane solutions. Who else tries to bribe an enemy rider while falling through the sky?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_scouts == 0 && v_b2_ch10_bribe != 2 || v_b2_ch10_scouts == 0 && v_ch10_gems != 1) {
"What if they have a tracking spell cast on us?"

"Like the ward the golden fox placed on us outside Thilias?" Daren shakes his head. "Unlikely. As a white mage, lingering enchantments stand out to me immediately. The fox is an ancient spirit whose weaves are nearly invisible, but orc shamans don't have that subtlety. I would catch their tracking runes within seconds."

"Your elementalists are landing," Nolderan says, looking up.

Flower and Kate drop their spells as they near the turf, landing lightly on the grass.

"Is the field clear?" Kate asks, looking around the smoking clearing. "Or is a third wave waiting in the timber?"

"Clear," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "I checked the clouds this time. After the slaughter they just took, they won't be back anytime soon."

"We should tend to our injuries regardless," Daren says, stepping forward with his hands glowing white. "Form up. I'll take each of you in turn."

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_scouts == 1 && v_b2_ch10_bribe != 2 || v_b2_ch10_scouts == 1 && v_ch10_gems != 1) {
"They could also have scouts, scattered all around the continent."

"That would explain the timing," Daren says. "Tyrath has thousands of orcs in his ranks. Scouts with transceivers could report our coordinates before we ever spot them. We have no choice but to break their vanguards until they realize the cost is too high."

"Your elementalists are landing," Nolderan says, looking up.

Flower and Kate drop their spells as they near the turf, landing lightly on the grass.

"Is the field clear?" Kate asks, looking around the smoking clearing. "Or is a third wave waiting in the timber?"

"Clear," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "I checked the clouds this time. After the slaughter they just took, they won't be back anytime soon."

"We should tend to our injuries regardless," Daren says, stepping forward with his hands glowing white. "Form up. I'll take each of you in turn."

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_bribe == 2 && v_ch10_gems == 1) {
As Kate and Flower set foot on the turf, the mercenary orc pulls his wyvern to a hover ten feet up, leathery wings beating a steady draft across the grass.

"The other riders are fed to the pines, human," the orc calls down. "My side of the bargain is complete."

"And here is mine," I say, pulling a cut diamond from my pouch and tossing it up.

The orc snatches the stone out of the air, holds it to the morning light to inspect the facets, and slips it into his sash.

"I take my leave before Tyrath's trackers notice my absence," the rider grunts. "May fortune favor your steel."

"Give Fyron our regards if you reach his camp," I call back.

The orc grunts in acknowledgment, wheels his mount around, and drives into a steep climb toward the western ridge.

"Is the field clear?" Kate asks, stepping over a severed branch. "Or is a third wave waiting in the timber?"

"Clear," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "I checked the clouds this time. After the slaughter they just took, they won't be back anytime soon."

"We should tend to our injuries regardless," Daren says, stepping forward with his hands glowing white. "Form up. I'll take each of you in turn."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Milady, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Milady)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Milady
TEXT:

Daren spends the next five minutes channeling white light into our cuts and bruises, sealing Hadrik's swollen black eye until the skin clears. As he finishes, the zipper on Melindra's tent rasps open.

She steps out in her silk clothes, smoothing her sleeves as if waking from a long nap. The moment she clears the flap, the air currents supporting my boots vanish, settling me flat onto the grass.

"Look who decided to join the waking world," Kate mutters.

"Melindra!" Flower calls out, waving energetically. "You missed the whole battle! And Daren's old master is here. This is Nolderan."

"Another companion?" Nolderan asks, looking her over.

Melindra catches the question before anyone else can speak, her voice turning sweet and helpless as she glides toward us.

"I am merely a traveling maiden trying to reach my ailing father," she says, offering a graceful, wide smile. "These generous warriors offered to escort me safely home."

She extends a slender hand toward Nolderan, inviting a formal greeting. "A pleasure, sir."

"Likewise, my lady," Nolderan says, taking her fingers and bowing slightly to kiss the back of her hand. "Where is your hometown?"

"A modest village," Melindra says smoothly. "Doubtful you'd know the name."

"We're taking her to Ollendor," Daren says helpfully.

Melindra's smile freezes. She cuts a sharp glare at Daren, though he remains completely oblivious as he wipes down his broadsword.

"Ollendor?" Nolderan perks up. "We skirted the outer perimeter on our way here. It so happens my mercenary company has a specialist who casts long-distance teleportation. If you come with me to our camp, I can arrange for him to jump you straight to the city gates. Shaves three days off the march. What do you say, Daren? Want to meet the company?"

"I... suppose that would save considerable time," Daren says, looking around at the rest of us. "Would everyone agree to taking the jump to Ollendor?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10a-Participant, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Participant)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Participant
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1) {
"If it saves three days of tramping through the brush, I'm all for it," Hadrik says.

"No objection," Leila writes in floating blue script.

"Neither do I," Kate says, turning to Nolderan with a rare softening of her posture. "I should apologize for my hostility earlier. You stood with us against the vanguards and that beast. You've earned our trust."

"Think nothing of it," Nolderan says with an easy wave. "If no one objects, let's break camp without delay. We shouldn't linger in a clearing where more summoned horrors might materialize."

"Agreed," Daren says. "Tear down the tents. We rally here in five minutes."

"Barry, darling!" Melindra calls out with an exaggerated, brittle smile. "A quick word in private, if you don't mind. Something vital."

Kate shoots me an icy glare before turning sharply toward her tent. Daren gives a slow shake of his head and marches away to roll his bedroll.

"What is it, Melindra?" I ask, stepping across the grass as the others move out of earshot.

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer != 1) {
"If it saves three days of tramping through the brush, I'm all for it," Hadrik says.

"No objection," Leila writes in floating blue script.

"Neither do I," Kate says, turning to Nolderan with a rare softening of her posture. "I should apologize for my hostility earlier. You stood with us against the vanguards and that beast. You've earned our trust."

"Think nothing of it," Nolderan says with an easy wave. "If no one objects, let's break camp without delay. We shouldn't linger in a clearing where more summoned horrors might materialize."

"Agreed," Daren says. "Tear down the tents. We rally here in five minutes."

"Barry, darling!" Melindra calls out with an exaggerated, brittle smile. "A quick word in private, if you don't mind. Something vital."

"What is it, Melindra?" I ask, stepping across the grass as the others move out of earshot.

}
"Barry," Melindra murmurs, her voice dropping into a tense whisper. "Mind explaining why everyone in this camp just agreed to walk blindly into an ambush?"

"An ambush?" I ask. "You think Nolderan is setting us up?"

"He's a tournament contestant!" she snaps. "Why wouldn't he want you dead?"

"For starters, he's Daren's old teacher," I say. "If he wanted us in the dirt, all he had to do was slip into the woods when the orcs surrounded the camp. We gave him the opening to walk away, and he stayed to fight a thirty-ton mammoth. What does he gain by bleeding for us?"

"I don't know his angle," Melindra says, crossing her arms. "I don't read minds. But my instincts are screaming that something is wrong. I'm not saying we turn down the teleportation. Gods know I'd rather appear at Ollendor's gates than hike through three more days of mud. But you're walking in with your shields down. Keep your eyes open."

choice(""I guess we could ask Arraka to regularly check for traps, on our way to Nolderan's camp."", B2-Ch10a-Intimidating, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Intimidating, v_b2_ch10_traps = 0)
choice(""I think you're worrying over nothing. Nolderan seems alright in my book."", B2-Ch10a-Intimidating, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10a-Intimidating, v_b2_ch10_traps = 1)


ID: B2-Ch10a-Intimidating
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_traps == 0) {
"I guess we could ask Arraka to regularly check for traps, on our way to Nolderan's camp."

"And I'll sweep the area with my magical sense for hidden casters," Melindra says. "I still don't understand why everyone opened their arms to a man they met an hour ago. The group treats me like an invasive pest, but this old swordsman walks into camp and he's practically family. What makes him so special?"

"Maybe if you told them about your wind-control problem, they wouldn't treat you like an outsider," I say. "If the group can't count on you when blood starts spilling, of course they're going to keep their distance. Why are you guarding that secret so desperately? What do you think they'll do if they find out? Try to murder you?"

"As a matter of fact, yes!" Melindra's eyes flash.

"You really believe that?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_traps == 1) {
"I think you're worrying over nothing. Nolderan seems alright in my book."

"Unbelievable," Melindra scoffs under her breath. "The trust you give away to strangers is ridiculous. Everyone here still treats me like an invasive pest, but this old swordsman walks into camp and he's practically family. What makes him so special?"

"Maybe if you told them about your wind-control problem, they wouldn't treat you like an outsider," I say. "If the group can't count on you when blood starts spilling, of course they're going to keep their distance. Why are you guarding that secret so desperately? What do you think they'll do if they find out? Try to murder you?"

"As a matter of fact, yes!" Melindra's eyes flash.

"You really believe that?"

}
"Because it's already happened!" Melindra says, her voice trembling with genuine venom. "Centuries ago, I traveled with a company for a short stretch. When they realized I couldn't regulate my output, they tried to 'fix' me with a white-mage limiter spell to cap my reservoir. In theory, it suppresses human mages safely. On a stillwater aura, a limiter can rip your channels to shreds. I explained the danger, and they ignored every word. The bastards crept into my tent while I was sleeping and started channeling the weave over my chest. If I hadn't woken in time to throw them through the trees, they would have snuffed out my life."

"Daren wouldn't force a limiter on you in your sleep," I say. "Not if you explained the physiological risk."

"Really?" Melindra's jaw tightens. "Your pack forced a lethal enchanter's oath around my neck before letting me walk beside you. You expect me to believe you'd prioritize my safety over group security because I asked politely?"

"I've known about your wind control for days," I say calmly. "Have I whispered a single word of it to Daren or Kate?"

#if(v_b2_ch10_toast == 1 && v_b2_ch10_traps == 0 && v_b2_ch9_drink == 1) {
Melindra goes quiet, chewing on her lip as she looks past my shoulder. "No. You haven't. But I'm keeping it quiet until we reach Ollendor. I can't offer much in an open clash anyway, so there's no reason to air the truth. If they hate me for sitting on the sidelines, I can live with it."

"Suit yourself," I say. "Let's get packed."

We pull down the canvas, roll our bedrolls, and hoist our packs. Nolderan leads the way into the timber, promising the mercenary camp sits less than half an hour away. The adrenaline of the mammoth clash has burned the last traces of dwarven ale from my veins, leaving my head clear.

Before setting off, Melindra and I had a quiet word with Arraka, tasking the banshee with screening the path for arcane wards. As we hike through the pines, we pass half a dozen scattered mages stuffing their satchels with dry pinecones. The moment they spot our weapons, they freeze and back away into the brush, eyes wide with terror.

"Why were those gatherers so frightened?" Daren asks, glancing back over his shoulder.

"We're heavily armed and traveling in force," Hadrik says, adjusting his pack straps. "They probably think we're going to shake them down for their pinecones."

}
